WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:04.520
When actors have a relationship off camera,&nbsp;
it can make things awkward on camera,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:08.440
which the stars of a certain low-budget&nbsp;
'90s movie know better than anybody. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:12.880
that's not the only time cinematic love&nbsp;
scenes have gotten majorly uncomfortable.

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:17.400
Love scenes aren't just for romantic comedies.&nbsp;
They can appear in all sorts of movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:21.920
even the most serious of serious dramas.&nbsp;
Quite frankly, audiences can often be in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:25.640
mood for some kind of romantic interlude&nbsp;
just to break the tension. Then again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.640 --> 00:00:28.720
just because a movie can have a love&nbsp;
scene doesn't mean that it should.

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:33.360
Case in point: the 2005 historical drama&nbsp;
Munich. If there's any movie that doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:38.160
need a steamy encounter, it's almost certainly&nbsp;
one about Israeli assassins sent to avenge the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:42.680
deaths of athletes who were killed by&nbsp;
terrorists at the 1972 Summer Olympics.

00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:47.680
After spending a very long time hunting down&nbsp;
terrorists, Avner, played by Eric Bana, has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:52.480
sex with his wife. The action cuts between their&nbsp;
wild ecstasy and violent images of people being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:57.280
gunned down at an airport. To be fair, director&nbsp;
Steven Spielberg was probably just trying to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:01.840
both artful and cathartic with this scene. And&nbsp;
after all, it's not like intercutting sex with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:07.400
violence hasn't been done before. But that doesn't&nbsp;
make it any less uncomfortable in this case.

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:11.920
After three movies about an evil doll possessed by&nbsp;
the soul of a serial killer, the team behind the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:16.600
Child's Play franchise needed to expand their&nbsp;
universe. And so, they decided to have Chucky&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:21.800
take a lover. 1998's Bride of Chucky finds the&nbsp;
titular killer doll reconnecting with Tiffany,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:26.000
his girlfriend from his time as a human.&nbsp;
After he kills her, he then transfers her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:30.468
soul into the body of a bride doll that looks&nbsp;
like a blond, female version of himself.

00:01:30.468 --> 00:01:33.280
A bloody crime spree from&nbsp;
these two psychopaths follows,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:37.520
and that isn't exactly a good environment&nbsp;
for a love scene. But this moment was always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:41.560
going to be strange and uncomfortable, as it&nbsp;
involves the most intimate acts rendered with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:45.560
the best technology and puppetry that&nbsp;
a mid-budget '90s movie had to offer.

00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:49.600
After Chucky proposes marriage, amorous&nbsp;
feelings quickly take over. There's a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:53.920
of loud open-mouth kissing from two characters&nbsp;
whose mouths don't really move, as well as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:58.240
stripping in front of a roaring fire and the two&nbsp;
dolls doing the deed in a silhouette that leaves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:03.680
little to the imagination. Making things even&nbsp;
more unbearable are all the cheesy wisecracks.

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:08.748
"I am anatomically correct, you know."

00:02:08.748 --> 00:02:10.888
"Ohhhh."

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:15.800
Few films have received the level of critical&nbsp;
derision and simultaneous fan acclaim suffered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:19.480
by Fifty Shades of Grey. That's probably&nbsp;
because most of the people who like the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:23.756
movie are also fans of the novels that&nbsp;
feature infamously corny sentences like,

00:02:23.756 --> 00:02:26.960
"My inner goddess is doing the&nbsp;
merengue with some salsa moves."

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:30.520
So it's no surprise then that the movie&nbsp;
version presented the same heavy-handed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.520 --> 00:02:34.440
implausible romance that sees mousy&nbsp;
virgin Anastasia Steele transformed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:37.280
into a kinky submissive at&nbsp;
the hands of Christian Grey.

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:41.160
Each love scene escalates further and further&nbsp;
into ridiculousness to the point that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:45.200
climax of the film occurs when Anastasia&nbsp;
freaks out after Christian spanks her too&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:50.200
hard. This happens in his so-called "play&nbsp;
room," which features hooks, whips, chains,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:55.240
and all manner of medieval-looking torture devices&nbsp;
that Anastasia seemingly has no issue with.

00:02:55.240 --> 00:02:58.960
"Stop! No!"

00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:02.920
The 2009 sci-fi horror movie Splice&nbsp;
raises a lot of questions about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:07.520
moral implications and personal ramifications&nbsp;
of science, particularly the rapidly evolving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:12.640
discipline of genetic engineering. While working&nbsp;
at a scientific facility called Nucleic Exchange&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:18.040
Research and Development, genetic engineers Clive&nbsp;
and Elsa, played by Adrien Brody and Sarah Polley,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.040 --> 00:03:22.720
develop a viable creature by mixing human&nbsp;
DNA with genetic materials from animals.

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:28.000
Elsa names this creature "Dren," which is "nerd"&nbsp;
backwards, and then she grows from a child to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:33.040
young woman very quickly. Clive clearly finds Dren&nbsp;
to be very beautiful, although she's more animal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:37.640
than human with her giant eyes, long tail,&nbsp;
and feet that look like hands. It's obvious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.640 --> 00:03:42.000
that we're building to some kind of intimate act&nbsp;
between Clive and Dren, but that doesn't make it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:47.200
any less weird when it happens. And it does indeed&nbsp;
happen, in a barn where Dren has been locked up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:53.080
no less. It's basically like Clive is getting&nbsp;
busy with his coworker and his pet all at once.

00:03:53.080 --> 00:03:57.600
MacGruber began as a recurring bit on Saturday&nbsp;
Night Live that parodied the action-adventure TV&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:02.120
show MacGyver, which starred Richard Dean Anderson&nbsp;
as a genius who could save the day by making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.120 --> 00:04:07.840
life-saving inventions out of household materials.&nbsp;
But in the SNL version, Will Forte plays MacGruber&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:13.280
as a supposed genius who would try to do the same&nbsp;
thing but inevitably always fail explosively.

00:04:13.280 --> 00:04:17.400
When MacGruber became a movie in 2010, it&nbsp;
needed to be fleshed out significantly for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:20.960
a feature-length running time, so it&nbsp;
wound up as a parody of over-the-top&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:25.360
'80s action movies. Amidst all the&nbsp;
explosions and lone-wolf theatrics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:29.640
those films usually also include a&nbsp;
steamy love scene, and so does MacGruber.

00:04:29.640 --> 00:04:33.880
As Mr. Mister's power ballad "Broken Wings"&nbsp;
plays, MacGruber begins to make sweet love&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.880 --> 00:04:38.720
to his wife Casey in her wedding gown. But&nbsp;
then things get hilariously weird and dark.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:42.760
The music falls away, and viewers see a&nbsp;
bare-bottomed MacGruber grunting away in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.760 --> 00:04:47.280
a graveyard while a groundskeeper watches.&nbsp;
Casey actually died earlier in the movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:51.160
which is re-established with a cut back&nbsp;
to MacGruber, who's now standing nude in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.160 --> 00:04:55.640
a graveyard at night, moaning and talking&nbsp;
dirty to a ghost that nobody else can see.

00:04:55.640 --> 00:04:57.880
"Just tell me what you want me to f---!"

00:04:57.880 --> 00:05:02.640
The twisty 2003 Korean thriller Oldboy&nbsp;
from writer-director Park Chan-wook&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.640 --> 00:05:06.720
won numerous awards on the festival circuit&nbsp;
and eventually became an international cult&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:10.760
favorite. It's not only one of the most&nbsp;
acclaimed South Korean films ever made,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.760 --> 00:05:15.200
it also helped put the country's cinema on the&nbsp;
map. Without Oldboy, Parasite may never have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:19.640
become an Oscar-winning phenomenon. In the midst&nbsp;
of the twisty and unpredictable plot, there's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.640 --> 00:05:24.120
love scene that becomes disturbing in retrospect&nbsp;
after all the mysteries have fully unraveled.

00:05:24.120 --> 00:05:28.640
In the opening scenes, the protagonist Oh Dae-su&nbsp;
is arrested one night for public drunkenness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.640 --> 00:05:31.720
which causes him to miss his young&nbsp;
daughter's birthday party. After a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.720 --> 00:05:35.880
friend retrieves him from the police station,&nbsp;
he's kidnapped and isolated in a hotel room&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.880 --> 00:05:40.520
for 15 years. When he's finally released, he&nbsp;
wanders into a sushi restaurant and strikes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.520 --> 00:05:44.640
up a relationship with young chef Mi-do.&nbsp;
He tries to get his life back together,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:49.160
though he gives up on finding his daughter when he&nbsp;
learns that she was adopted by a Swedish couple.

00:05:49.160 --> 00:05:53.960
Then his captor Lee Woo-jin contacts Dae-su&nbsp;
and gives him an ultimatum. If he guesses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.960 --> 00:05:58.200
the reason for his imprisonment within&nbsp;
five days, Woo-jin will kill himself;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.200 --> 00:06:03.520
if not, he'll kill Mi-do. Dae-su and Mi-do's&nbsp;
relationship grows increasingly intimate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.520 --> 00:06:07.040
and Dae-su soon figures it all out.&nbsp;
He went to high school with Woo-jin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.040 --> 00:06:11.760
and he saw him engage in incest with his sister.&nbsp;
Dae-su spread the news, and the sister was so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.760 --> 00:06:16.280
mortified that she killed herself. It would seem&nbsp;
that Woo-jin has had his revenge on Dae-su with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.280 --> 00:06:21.040
the years-long imprisonment. But as it turns&nbsp;
out, his plan was even more sinister than that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.040 --> 00:06:26.680
as the climactic scene revealed that Dae-su's&nbsp;
long-lost daughter is actually Mi-do.

00:06:26.680 --> 00:06:31.160
Before he became an Oscar winner for Green Book,&nbsp;
Peter Farrelly and his brother Bobby made some of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.160 --> 00:06:35.560
the most outrageous comedies of the '90s, like&nbsp;
There's Something About Mary, Dumb and Dumber,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.560 --> 00:06:40.600
and Kingpin. That last one stars Woody Harrelson&nbsp;
as down-on-his-luck former bowling champion Roy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.600 --> 00:06:45.760
Munsen. An attempt to hustle amateur bowlers goes&nbsp;
poorly, and he loses his hand in a ball return&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.760 --> 00:06:49.800
accident. Two decades later, he's reduced to&nbsp;
selling bowling supplies and spending most of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.800 --> 00:06:54.440
time drinking. He has little choice but to get in&nbsp;
bed with his landlady, played by frequent Farrelly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.440 --> 00:06:59.400
collaborator Lin Shaye, so that she'll be more&nbsp;
forgiving with his perpetually late rent payments.

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:03.880
That's the uncomfortable setup: a man who suffers&nbsp;
from excessive drinking use has to prostitute&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.880 --> 00:07:08.600
himself to avoid becoming homeless. There's&nbsp;
an even more unsettling payoff. Roy stages an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.600 --> 00:07:12.840
attack on the landlady so as to chase the guy&nbsp;
off and earn himself some favor. But she finds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.840 --> 00:07:17.160
out about it and then forces him to make it up&nbsp;
to her in the bedroom. Then it cuts to her lying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.160 --> 00:07:22.440
in bed smoking a cigarette in post-coital bliss&nbsp;
while Roy throws up in the adjoining bathroom.

00:07:22.440 --> 00:07:24.960
"You still owe me another month's rent."

00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:28.880
Almost any love scene in a comedy could make&nbsp;
this list, as they're usually played for awkward&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.880 --> 00:07:34.000
laughs. But 2009's Observe and Report isn't your&nbsp;
standard comedy. It's a dark and twisted flick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.000 --> 00:07:38.720
written and directed by Jody Hill, the creator&nbsp;
of the HBO series Eastbound &amp; Down. When mall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.720 --> 00:07:43.240
security officer Ronnie, played by Seth Rogen,&nbsp;
finally lands his dream girl Brandi, played by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.240 --> 00:07:47.800
Anna Faris, their romantic encounter is rather&nbsp;
seedy. They're both drunk, which already means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.800 --> 00:07:51.920
that they're not thinking straight and can't give&nbsp;
informed consent. Nevertheless, they go back to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.920 --> 00:07:56.680
her place for sex, even though Brandi has ingested&nbsp;
a lot of clonazepam and is only semi-conscious.

00:07:56.680 --> 00:07:59.600
Observe and Report tries to&nbsp;
recover this scene with a joke,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.600 --> 00:08:03.800
as the passed-out Brandi wakes up and asks&nbsp;
Ronnie why he's stopping. But at that point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.800 --> 00:08:08.200
the creepiness is just too much, and we're&nbsp;
witnessing a gross exploitation of a terrible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.200 --> 00:08:13.040
situation. Although considering how dark this&nbsp;
movie is, that may have been intentional.

00:08:13.040 --> 00:08:18.080
The 1995 erotic thriller Showgirls came hot on&nbsp;
the heels of director Paul Verhoeven's iconic run&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.080 --> 00:08:23.600
of RoboCop, Total Recall, and Basic Instinct. So&nbsp;
he was probably looking for a similarly visceral&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.600 --> 00:08:28.360
response when he took on the world of showgirl&nbsp;
dancers in Las Vegas. Unfortunately, he made a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.360 --> 00:08:33.800
film that's had a rocky reception at best. Some&nbsp;
critics cited its campy melodrama as a detraction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.800 --> 00:08:37.600
And sure, if you're not on the film's unusual&nbsp;
wavelength, it may feel like something that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.600 --> 00:08:42.880
would be more at home on Lifetime, if not for the&nbsp;
graphic sex, particularly the infamous pool scene.

00:08:42.880 --> 00:08:47.400
Elizabeth Berkley plays the lead character of&nbsp;
Nomi Malone, who writhes around on top of Kyle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.400 --> 00:08:51.720
MacLachlan like someone just threw a toaster&nbsp;
into the water. If you can manage to wipe away&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.720 --> 00:08:55.520
your tears of laughter, you can literally&nbsp;
see all the stunned confusion written all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.520 --> 00:09:00.400
over his face. But Verhoeven's reputation has&nbsp;
endured beyond Showgirls, and the film itself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.400 --> 00:09:05.489
has since been rediscovered and reevaluated&nbsp;
by a new generation of audiences and critics.

00:09:05.489 --> 00:09:07.169
"It doesn't suck."

00:09:07.520 --> 00:09:11.760
1997's Kickboxing Academy was also&nbsp;
released under the far more boring,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.760 --> 00:09:15.160
if accurate, title "Teen Boxer."&nbsp;
The plot, naturally enough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.160 --> 00:09:18.960
concerns the day-to-day activities at a&nbsp;
kickboxing academy. Ultimately, the kids&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.960 --> 00:09:22.600
at the academy have to square off against&nbsp;
the kids from another martial arts school.

00:09:22.600 --> 00:09:26.400
Among those kickboxing kids are a couple&nbsp;
of fresh-faced teenagers named Cindy and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.400 --> 00:09:30.480
Danny. They're played by Chyler Leigh, years&nbsp;
before she starred in Not Another Teen Movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.480 --> 00:09:34.160
and Grey's Anatomy, and Christopher Khayman&nbsp;
Lee, who's probably best known for multiple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.160 --> 00:09:37.840
Power Rangers series. In real life,&nbsp;
the two of them are brother and sister,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.840 --> 00:09:42.080
which makes the fact that their characters are&nbsp;
romantically involved the height of awkwardness.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.080 --> 00:09:45.640
And the people making Kickboxing Academy&nbsp;
apparently didn't think it was too weird&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.640 --> 00:09:50.440
to include multiple scenes in which Cindy&nbsp;
and Danny passionately kiss each other.

00:09:50.440 --> 00:09:53.880
At the end of 2000's Requiem for a&nbsp;
Dream, each of the main characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.880 --> 00:09:58.280
are deep in the worst chapters of their&nbsp;
addictions. Jennifer Connelly plays Marion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.280 --> 00:10:02.200
a heroin addict who resorts to selling her&nbsp;
body for the drug when she's recruited into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.200 --> 00:10:07.160
doing a show for a bunch of salivating men who&nbsp;
jeer and throw money at her and another woman.

00:10:07.160 --> 00:10:11.200
To be fair, this isn't so much a love scene as&nbsp;
it is a horrifically graphic act that occurs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.200 --> 00:10:15.440
amid a swirling, brilliantly edited sequence&nbsp;
that weaves together the awful predicaments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.440 --> 00:10:19.880
that each character has found themselves in.&nbsp;
On top of that, it also imparts their panic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.880 --> 00:10:23.949
and despair onto the audience through a&nbsp;
jarringly effective use of shock value.

